  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAn exiting closed upper low today, followed by a swift-moving\ntrough late Sunday into Monday morning, may drive periods of\nlake-enhanced light rain showers for portions of northwest PA.\nAdditional tightening of the surface gradient with the second\nwave yield increased afternoon wind gusts that may see portions\nof eastern Tucker County approach Wind Advisory criteria.\n\nOutside of these weather features, the prevailing outlook\nthrough midweek sees height rises and building high pressure\nthat promotes dry weather and temperature rising about 5 to 10\ndegrees above normal by Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nEnsemble model guidance has fairly high certainty in the\ndevelopment of the next upper level trough starting Wednesday\nwith a lobe of energy digging south out of Canada. But starting\nWednesday night and beyond, large variances in the\ndevelopment/positioning of this system is resulting in notable\nswings in potential outcomes. For example, by Saturday, current\nensemble cluster analysis suggest that the upper trough axis \ncould either be exiting the Atlantic Coast, OR an nearly cutoff\nlow sits over the eastern Tennessee River Valley. Until closer\nalignment of outcomes is seen, little actionable weather\nassessments can be provided.\n\nThe broad strokes of what can be said is the transition to an\nactive pattern Thursday through at least Saturday that may\ninclude multiple periods of precipitation while likely pushing\narea temperature below normal. The latter point also means that\nscenarios do exist for the first accumulating snow of the\nseason, though mainly for northwest PA and the higher terrain.",
